        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "A hydrogen halide adds across the C=C bond: H goes to one carbon and the halogen to the other. With propene, HBr can give 2-bromopropane (major) or 1-bromopropane (minor), and HCl can give 2-chloropropane (major) or 1-chloropropane (minor). The question asks what <i>could</i> be produced, so minor products count.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:14.2(2)",
                "syllabus:14.2(5)"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Each addition puts only one halogen atom on the molecule, because one H and one X add across the single C=C bond. A product with both Br and Cl, such as 1-bromo-2-chloropropane, would need BrCl to add, and no BrCl is present.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:14.2(2)"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "optionAnalysis": [
            {
              "key": "A",
              "correct": false,
              "text": "1-bromo-2-chloropropane has two halogen atoms; adding HX puts H on one carbon, so only one halogen can be added."
            },
            {
              "key": "B",
              "correct": true,
              "text": "1-bromopropane is the minor (anti-Markovnikov) HBr product and 2-chloropropane the major HCl product; both can form."
            },
            {
              "key": "C",
              "correct": false,
              "text": "2-chloropropane also forms: it is the major product of HCl addition."
            },
            {
              "key": "D",
              "correct": false,
              "text": "1-bromopropane can also form, as the minor product of HBr addition via the less stable primary carbocation."
            }
          ],
